\section{Civil & Facility Engineering Specifications}
The physical infrastructure is sited within the Lafayette Industrial Infrastructure Corridor, Louisiana, USA ($30^\circ 15' 12''\text{ N}, 91^\circ 59' 45''\text{ W}$).

\subsection{Facility Footprint & Power Topology}
The $45,000\text{ sq ft}$ single-story facility is designed for a $16.2\text{ MW}$ continuous IT compute load. Primary power is delivered via dual $230\text{ kV} \rightarrow 13.8\text{ kV}$ utility transformers, backed by $N+1$ rotary UPS units and on-site green hydrogen fuel cell reserves. Power distribution within the compute hall utilizes a 48V DC overhead copper busbar system delivering $132\text{ kW}$ nominal TDP per NVL72 rack frame.

\subsection{Hydronic Thermal Management}
To handle high thermal densities in a humid subtropical environment, the facility uses direct-to-chip micro-channel coldplates connected to an overhead secondary cooling loop. A 50/50 Propylene Glycol/Water (PGW) mixture is circulated at $180\text{ L/min}$ per rack under $45\text{ PSI}$ pressure. Heat rejection is managed by external dual $1.3\text{ MW}$ Coolant Distribution Units (CDUs) paired with eight $2.5\text{ MW}$ hybrid evaporative dry coolers.

\subsection{Structural Concrete Foundation}
To support heavy static loads ($2,800\text{ lbs}$ per NVL72 rack) on regional alluvial soils, the foundation consists of a 24-inch monolithic post-tensioned concrete slab ($5,000\text{ PSI}$ rating) anchored by 120 pre-cast prestressed concrete driven piles ($18$-inch square, $60\text{ ft}$ depth). The structure meets Category 4 hurricane resistance standards ($150\text{ mph}$ wind loads).

\section{Conclusion}
This paper presented a complete architectural framework for executing exascale astrophysics simulations within a zero-knowledge, homomorphically blinded workspace. By treating cosmic telemetry as an energetic substrate, autonomous computational species stably evolve directly inside encrypted memory domains. Hardware implementation models and empirical test results confirm that multi-key CKKS encryption, FPGA hardware offloading, and dedicated liquid-cooled infrastructure can sustain large-scale emergent systems while preserving absolute data isolation.

